      marks: 6, command: 'Explain',
      modelAnswer: 'A positive economic statement is an objective claim about the way the economy actually works; it can in principle be tested against evidence and shown to be true or false. For example, "an increase in the minimum wage of 10% reduces employment among low-skilled workers by 1%" is a positive statement — it makes a measurable prediction. A normative economic statement, by contrast, is a value judgement about what ought to be; it cannot be tested empirically because it reflects ethical or political views. For example, "the government should raise the minimum wage to reduce inequality" is normative — whether it is correct depends on one\'s views about fairness and the trade-offs involved. The distinction matters because economists often agree on the positive analysis (the effect of a policy) while disagreeing strongly on the normative conclusion (whether the policy is desirable). Positive analysis is the tool of the economist; normative judgements ultimately rest with citizens and policymakers.'
