      modelAnswer: 'Economists classify productive resources into four factors of production, each earning a different reward. Land refers to all natural resources — the soil, minerals, oil, water, forests and the physical sites on which production takes place. The reward to land is rent. Labour is the human effort, both physical and mental, used in production; this includes manual workers, professionals and managers. The reward to labour is wages (or salaries). Capital is the manufactured aids to production — machinery, factories, computers, vehicles, infrastructure — that are themselves produced and then used to produce other goods and services. Capital should be distinguished from financial capital (money used to buy capital goods). The reward to capital is interest. Enterprise is the willingness of entrepreneurs to take risks by organising the other three factors, innovating and bearing the uncertainty of producing for a market. The reward to enterprise is profit. Together, these four factors are combined in production processes; the relative quantities and qualities of each determine an economy\'s productive capacity. Modern economists sometimes add human capital — the skills and knowledge workers possess — as either a fifth factor or a part of labour, recognising its central role in productivity in advanced economies.'
